questions.stem:
A river log needs the position of the first s in a place name, and the program below reads it out. What value is printed? Respond with the number only.
String river = "mississippi";
System.out.print(river.indexOf('s'));

A pebble-pattern routine locates a doubled letter and then reports the character just inside the pair. What prints?
String s = "pebble";
int i = s.indexOf("bb");
System.out.print(s.charAt(i + 1));

A pair-hunting routine finds na in banana, then searches again from just after that hit. What prints?
String s = "banana";
int first = s.indexOf("na");
int again = s.indexOf("na", first + 1);
System.out.print(first + ":" + again);

A log summariser reaches for the last time a spoken phrase appeared in a name. What value is printed? Respond with the number only.
String log = "abracadabra";
System.out.print(log.lastIndexOf("abra"));

A name check asks whether a rare two-letter tag is present at all, and prints whatever comes back. What value is printed? Respond with the number only.
String name = "harbor";
System.out.print(name.lastIndexOf("zz"));

A scanner refuses to look further along than a cut-off point when hunting for a pair. What prints?
String s = "banana";
int at = s.lastIndexOf("na", 3);
System.out.print(at);

A labelling routine slices the middle of a bird name for a shelf card. What prints?
String s = "albatross";
System.out.print(s.substring(2, 5));

A badge printer keeps only the numeric tail of a volunteer record. What prints?
String tag = "volunteer-42";
System.out.print(tag.substring(9));

A receipt wants to show a possibly empty remainder beside a name, and the code below brackets it. What does the last line print, and why?
String s = "owl";
String tail = s.substring(3);
System.out.println("[" + tail + "]");

A sign routine slices a bay name and then shouts the result in capitals. What prints?
String s = "quietbay";
System.out.print(s.substring(3, 8).toUpperCase());

A text tool tries four ways of cutting a short word. Which of the statements below run to completion without throwing? Select all that apply.
class Note {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "owl";
        //FILL
    }
}

A release tag looks like v1.2.3, and the code already locates both dots. Fill the blank so the printed value is 3.
String tag = "v1.2.3";
int dot = tag.indexOf('.');
int next = tag.indexOf('.', dot + 1);
String last = tag.substring(___);
System.out.print(last);
// prints: 3

A packing label drops a prefix and then reports the first letter of what remains. What prints?
String s = "container";
String tail = s.substring(4);
System.out.print(tail.charAt(0));

A cipher shifts a letter forward by one code unit. What prints?
String code = "abc";
System.out.print(code.charAt(1) + 1);

A checksum adds the first two letters of a greeting as numbers. What value is printed? Respond with the number only.
String s = "hi";
System.out.print(s.charAt(0) + s.charAt(1));

A login check ignores the initial and asks whether the final letter of what remains is r. Fill the blank so the printed value is true.
String user = "samir";
boolean endsInR = user.substring(1).charAt(___) == 'r';
System.out.print(endsInR);
// prints: true

A badge reader should report the final character of a code, but the program crashes at runtime instead of printing anything. Which replacement for the marked line makes it print last=9?
String badge = "A19";
char last = badge.charAt(badge.length());//FIX
System.out.print("last=" + last);
// prints: last=9

A report window cuts a string with equal bounds, and the next line reaches into the result. What happens on the charAt call, and why?
String s = "abcdef";
String middle = s.substring(3, 3);
System.out.print("[" + middle + "]");
char c = middle.charAt(0);

A planting list keeps two names and wants the one that comes first in a dictionary. What prints?
String a = "maple";
String b = "oak";
String first = a.compareTo(b) < 0 ? a : b;
System.out.print(first);

A locker check asks whether two codes are the same word and prints the test. What prints, and what rule explains it?
String a = "Route66";
String b = "route66";
System.out.print(a.compareTo(b) == 0);

An ordering test must report true when the first fruit sorts after the second, yet it prints false. Which replacement for the marked line prints true?
String a = "pear";
String b = "fig";
boolean after = a.compareTo(b) == 1;//FIX
System.out.print(after);
// prints: true

A fruit bowl wants the alphabetically earlier of two names kept in first. Fill the blank so the printed value is kumquat.
String a = "kiwi";
String b = "kumquat";
String first;
if (b.compareTo(a) ___) {
    first = b;
} else {
    first = a;
}
System.out.print(first);
// prints: kumquat

A pattern tool picks every other letter out of a six-letter word and glues them into a new word. What prints?
String s = "abcdef";
String out = "";
for (int k = 0; k < s.length(); k = k + 2) {
    out = out + s.charAt(k);
}
System.out.print(out);

A counter writes its running values into one growing String as it climbs from 1 to 4. What prints?
String out = "";
for (int n = 1; n <= 4; n++) {
    out = out + n;
}
System.out.print(out);

The loop below builds the right answer, six characters of it. What is really happening inside the loop on each pass?
String out = "";
for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) {
    out = out + "ab";
}
System.out.print(out.length());

A builder is used to gather a code, and the program reports how much text has piled up. What prints?
StringBuilder b = new StringBuilder();
b.append(9);
b.append(10);
System.out.print(b.length());

A copy loop rebuilds a name but prints mapl, quietly losing the final letter. Which replacement for the marked loop header makes it print maple?
String s = "maple";
String out = "";
for (int k = 0; k < s.length() - 1; k++) {//FIX
    out = out + s.charAt(k);
}
System.out.print(out);
// prints: maple

A word is flipped with a builder and the result is assigned to a String. Fill the blank so the printed value is lasrod.
String s = "dorsal";
String reversed = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().___;
System.out.print(reversed);
// prints: lasrod
